// The look (docs/design.md, "What an author writes"): how light becomes the picture. The look owns
// tone, alone: matter and the sky say only how much light there is, physically, and the look says
// what the screen shows of it (exposure, a tone curve, a grade, later ink). Style enters nowhere
// else.
//
// The picture hands the look what it needs at each pixel (`Seen`). Today that is the light the
// pixel's ray brings back; what a look will want later (which way the surface there faces, how
// far it is, which thing it belongs to, how all of that turns from one pixel to the next, so a
// tone's or a line's edge comes out smooth) is added to `Seen` by the picture, and a look written
// against today's `Seen` keeps working.

/// What a pixel's ray brought back, as the picture hands it to the look.
pub struct Seen: Copy {
    /// The light arriving at the eye along the ray (radiance, linear, the sky's units).
    pub light: vec3,
}

/// A look: light to the colour the screen shows, linear (from 0, black, to 1, the screen's
/// white; the picture encodes it for the screen).
pub trait Look: Copy + GpuData {
    fn tone(self, seen: Seen) -> vec3
}
